// The core library: a bundle of the everyday words. Each topic is a library of its own,
// and `use core` uses them in this order.
//
// A line's shape decides first which def wraps the others: fixed words that
// surround other defs (`api ... runs ... and sends ...`, `is ... ?`), or a `written`
// sentence that starts the line (`all of ... are ...`). Otherwise order matters: a def
// written earlier is applied last, so it is the outer part of a line. That is why
// `a is 2 + 3 > 4` means `a is ((2 + 3) > 4)`. So the libraries are used loosest first.

// First the kernel's entries: the def language, the words entries are written with
// (entry), and the flags, statements and modifiers written with them. They make no
// defs a line is read with (but the kept lines' words), so their place doesn't change
// how a line is read.
use language/def
use entry
use flag/written
use flag/regex
use flag/later
use statement/comment
use statement/argument
use statement/indent
use statement/error
use statement/echo
use statement/scope
use modifier/shared
use modifier/internal
use loops
use questions
use errors
use values
use checks
use console
use joined
use logic
use comparison
use kinds
use tables
use math
use text
use lists
use formats
use time
use random
use number-words
use phrases
